"""
The first board's last layer, with one scale on its outputs.

pta_board_budget.py found most of the crosstalk's error to be a gain on
every output, measured by grx930's probe against the host's own network, and
a source corrected on the weights to take gain off.  It could not say what one
scale on the outputs would buy at the gate, because the gate holds the layer
to a reference in its declared format and nothing had been fitted against
that.  B19 put the question of an output scale off until this was run (this
plan's section 8, question 10).

This is it.  grx930's harness now gives the last layer's outputs one scale,
fixed at calibration: the least-squares slope of the declared reference on
the tile's outputs, over a thousand images the network was not trained on
(the first of the 6,000 it holds out), run through the same tile after the
test images, the light's shots their own.  It then gives the gate's figures
again with the outputs times that scale, and the scale the test images would
fit themselves, which no calibration knows and no scale betters.

MEASURED IN A MODEL, by grx930 (c930/doc/pta_error_model_design_note.md
section 5, "The first board's last layer, with one scale on its outputs",
2026-10-14; `SHAPE=chain sim/pta_mnist.sh DIR WORK chaingain`, `pta_mnist
eval --gatescale`).  pta_board_chain.py's networks, tile and draws, on 10,000
images and on the first thousand, which is the gate's own set; nine rows: the
format alone; the crosstalk alone and the shot noise alone; the tile as
budgeted, with each of those two left out, and with its range fitted; and the
tile as budgeted lit, at training's range and fitted.  Every run but those of
four rows on a thousand images is a run a file before this one holds, and the
scale leaves it as it was: grx930's mode holds each line, up to the scale's
figures, to the line the mode before it wrote.  This file holds the scale's
figures for every run, and the rest for the four rows that are new.

DERIVED here:

  a row's figures    which have to be the five tables grx930 printed, cell
                     for cell.  Every figure but the scale's, of every row
                     a file before this one holds, is worked out from that
                     file's
  what a scale buys  a row's rms error over the reference's rms with no
                     scale, less that with the scale, a mean over its runs
  how much is a      the share of a row's squared error the scale takes
  gain               off: one less the square of the ratio of the two

HOW SURE.  Five networks, 2.8 errors for one in twenty.  The scale is fixed
on other images than it is judged on, and the scale the test images would fit
themselves is given beside it: the two are within a hundredth in every run,
so the scale is a property of the tile and its calibration and not of the
images it was fixed on.

WHAT THIS IS NOT.  grxgpu's classifier, or a chip: everything
pta_board_chain.py says of itself.  A calibration a chip has: the scale is
applied to the outputs on the host's side, exactly, and fixed once a run;
writing it into the weights, or into the converter's codes, is not modelled.
A drifted tile, or one calibrated in a cycle.  A ruling on whether the
demonstration's reference may carry a scale, which is the owner's (B19).

Standard library only.  Run:  python3 docs/designs/pta_board_scale.py
"""
import contextlib
import io
import math
import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()):
    import pta_board_chain as chain
    import pta_board_range as fitted
    import pta_board_lit as lit
    import pta_board_budget as bud

MNIST, FASHION, INVERTED = chain.MNIST, chain.FASHION, chain.INVERTED
WORKLOADS, KINDS = chain.WORKLOADS, chain.KINDS
BEFORE, REFERENCE = chain.BEFORE, chain.REFERENCE
SEEDS, DRAWS, PLAN, T95 = chain.SEEDS, chain.DRAWS, chain.PLAN, chain.T95
ALL, FEW = chain.ALL, chain.FEW                # the test set, and its first thousand: the gate's own
GATE_RMS, GATE_MAX, GATE_CLASS = chain.GATE_RMS, chain.GATE_MAX, chain.GATE_CLASS
HOST, DECLARED_CLASS = chain.HOST, chain.DECLARED_CLASS
TRAIN, FIT = fitted.TRAIN, fitted.FIT          # the range training's rule gives, and fitted
LIT, LITFIT = lit.LIT, lit.LITFIT              # B16's source, read and corrected; and that fitted
CALIBRATION, FIRST = 1000, 54000               # the images the scale is fixed on, and the first of them
# the rows: (core, way, images); a core is bud's, the tile as budgeted with or without some of its rows
CORES = ("format", "xtalk", "shot", "less_xtalk", "less_shot", "budget")
ROWS = tuple(row + (images,) for images in (ALL, FEW)
             for row in tuple((c, TRAIN) for c in CORES) + (("budget", FIT), ("budget", LIT), ("budget", LITFIT)))
NAME = {"format": "the format alone", "xtalk": "the crosstalk alone", "shot": "the shot noise alone",
        "less_xtalk": "less the crosstalk", "less_shot": "less the shot noise", "budget": "as budgeted"}
WAY = {TRAIN: "", FIT: ", fitted", LIT: ", lit", LITFIT: ", lit, fitted"}
# a value of the scale's, by where it is in a run's cell
SC, SC_RMS, SC_RMSFS, SC_MAXFS, SC_AGREE, BEST, BEST_RMS = range(7)
assert len(ROWS) == 18 and DRAWS == (0, 1, 2, 3, 4)

